/*!
\page page_cryspy_modules Module Specification

[TOC]

Each AlamDMC module has its own module-specific configuration, which
is located in the trailing part of the configuration file.
Furthermore, each module will generate its own specific output data.

\section module_asr Multiaxial Stress Test Module (keyword: ASR)

The Multiaxial Stress Test Module prescribes a generic stress loading on a virtual sample.
Several subsequent deformation _steps_ may be imposed;
each step imposes a certain (constant) stress mode for a certain deformation (which may be infinitesimally small).

A specific _sample reference frame_ (with `x`-, `y`- and `z`-axes) is associated to the virtual sample.
The imposed stress state is described with reference to this sample reference frame.

\subsection configuration_asr Module-specific configuration

-# \confdef{sample_orientation,euler} specifies the orientation of the sample reference frame of the virtual specimen, in format specified in \ref euler_option.
-# \confdef{n_steps,integer} sets the number of subsequent deformation steps, each one being specified by the configuration settings 3.-4. and their optional subsettings.
These settings are repeated \conffield{n_steps} times in configuration file, in the same order as they are to be applied to the virtual sample.
-# \confdef{stress_mode,symsrtensor} specifies the imposed stress mode for given step, with reference to the sample (`xyz`) reference frame, and in format specified in \ref symsrtensor_option.
Note that the hydrostatic part of the imposed stress mode is ignored, as well as its norm.
-# \confdef{update_state,logical} is a switch that controls update of material state for given step. When set to
  - `False` <BR> then \conffield{stress_mode} is merely queried without finite deformation and without associated evolution of state.
  - `True` <BR> then a finite deformation has to be specified as follows:
    -# \conffield{incrementation_type} specifies how the deformation increments will be calculated. The only allowed values in this module are:
		`StrainTensor`, `StrainTensorIncrement` and `PlasticWork`. See \ref incrementation_option for details.
	-# \conffield{step_magnitude} provides maximal deformation (as specified by \conffield{incrementation_type}) to be reached in the virtual test.
	-# \conffield{increment_magnitude} sets the magnitude of deformation increments.

\par Example
Please see the configuration file `AKDQf_asr.cfg` from `examples` directory:
\includelineno AKDQf_asr.cfg

\subsection output_asr Module-specific output

The module-specific outputfile `<output_prefix>.asr` makes column-wise reporting of:
<ol>
	<li> \outdef{step} The deformation step sequence number
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{inc, increment_ASR} The increment sequence number of the current
	deformation step
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{epsilon_vM, eps_vM_ASR} The von Mises equivalent strain of the current step
	\f$ \epsilon_{vM,step} \f$

	It is derived from the strain tensor of current step \f$ \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{step} \f$ as:
	\f[
		\epsilon_{vM,total}=\sqrt{2/3} \| \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{total} \|
	\f]
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{sigma_vM} The von Mises equivalent stress  of the current increment. It is derived from the deviatoric stress tensor as follows:
	\f[
		\sigma_{vM,step}=\sqrt{3/2} \| \boldsymbol{\sigma}_{step} \|
	\f]
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{dotW} The rate of plastic work \f$ \dot{W} \f$
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{M} The volume-average Taylor factor \f$ \overline{M} \f$

	It is obtained from volume-averaging the sum of microscopic slip rates
	over all deformation systems \f$  \dot{\gamma} \f$, as
	\f[
		\overline{M}= \frac{\overline{ \dot{\gamma} }}{D_{vM}}
	\f]
	The von Mises equivalent strain rate \f$ D_{vM}=\sqrt{2/3} \| \mathbf{D} \| \f$ is
	used as normalization factor.
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{residual} The norm of the difference between the stress mode requested by the user and the stress mode obtained by the model.
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{sigma_xx} A component of the deviatoric stress tensor \f$ \mathbf{S} \f$
	in the sample (`xyz`) reference frame
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{sigma_yy} Similar to \ref sigma_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{sigma_zz} Similar to \ref sigma_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{sigma_xy} Similar to \ref sigma_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{sigma_yz} Similar to \ref sigma_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{sigma_xz} Similar to \ref sigma_xx
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{A_xx} A component of the strain mode tensor \f$ \mathbf{A} \f$ in
	the sample (`xyz`) reference frame
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_yy} Similar to \ref A_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_zz} Similar to \ref A_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_xy} Similar to \ref A_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_yz} Similar to \ref A_xx
	</li>
	<li> \outdef{A_xz} Similar to \ref A_xx
	</li>
</ol>

\section module_qrs Uniaxial Anisotropy Module (keyword: QRS)

The Uniaxial Anisotropy Module provides Lankford coefficients (in two variants: q-values and r-values; cf. below) and normalized uniaxial yield stresses (s-values),
for a specified range of in-plane directions, and under tensile loading. These properties are queried for the virtual microstructure in the given state without
any finite deformation imposed. There is no evolution of state associated with this module.

For each loading case, a _sample reference frame_ (with `x`-, `y`- and `z`-axes) is associated to the virtual sample.
The loading direction is aligned with the sample `x`-axis, by convention.
The `x`- and `y`-axes are restricted to the material `1`-`2`-plane.
Consequently, the sample `z`-axis is aligned with the material `3`-direction.

\subsection configuration_qrs Module-specific configuration

-# \confdef{angular_resolution, real} defines the angle in degrees between consecutive sampling directions in the xy-plane.
-# \conffield{pure_uniaxial_state} is defined [here](@ref pure_uniaxial_state_anchor).
-# \confdef{calculate_Mfactor,logical} is a flag to request calculation of average Taylor factor in module-specific output file. If `True`, it may
increase calculation time for certain model assumptions.
-# \confdef{fold_symmetry,logical} enables or disables averaging of output variables over symmetrically equivalent positions.

\par Example
Please see the configuration file `AKDQf_qrs.cfg` from `examples` directory:
\includelineno AKDQf_qrs.cfg

\subsection output_qrs Module-specific output

The module-specific outputfile `<output_prefix>.xqrs` makes column-wise reporting of:
<ol>
	<li> \outdef{angle} The in-plane orientation angle, as requested in \conffield{sample_orientation_range}
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{rho} The requested imposed stress ratio \f$ \rho \f$, as requested in \conffield{pure_uniaxial_state}. See also \ref stress_ratio_rho
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{q-value} The instantaneous q-value \f$ q_i \f$

	It is defined as
	\f[
		q_i = \frac{-D_{yy}}{D_{xx}}
	\f]
	where \f$D_{yy}\f$ and \f$D_{xx}\f$ are the strain rates in the width and tensile
	directions, respectively. It relates to the instantaneous r-value as \f$ q_i = \frac{r_i}{1+r_i} \f$.
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{r-value} The instantaneous r-value \f$ r_i \f$

	It is defined as
	\f[
		r_i = \frac{D_{yy}}{D_{zz}}
	\f]
	where \f$D_{yy}\f$ and \f$D_{zz}\f$ are the strain rates in the width and thickness
	directions, respectively. It relates to the instantaneous q-value as
	\f$ \displaystyle r_i = \frac{q_i}{1-q_i} \f$.
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{s-value} A normalised flow stress \f$ s \f$
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{sigma_xx} The normal component along the x-axis of the total stress
	\f$\sigma_{xx}\f$

	This component of the _total_ stress  \f$\boldsymbol{\sigma}\f$ is derived from
	the deviatoric stress tensor \f$\mathbf{S}\f$ as:
	\f[
		\sigma_{xx}=S_{xx}+\sigma_h=S_{xx}-S_{zz}
	\f]
	in which the hydrostatic stress \f$ \sigma_h \f$ is derived from the assumption that
	the sample is stress-free along the z-axis: \f$ \sigma_{zz}=S_{zz}+\sigma_h=0 \f$.
	</li>

	<li> \outref{M-factor}
	</li>

	<li> \outref{residual}
	</li>
</ol>

\section module_yld Yield Locus Module (keyword: YLD)

The Yield Locus Module calculates generic 2-dimensional sections of the yield locus.
There is no evolution of state associated with this module.

\subsection configuration_yld Module-specific configuration

-# \confdef{angular_resolution,real} Angle between sample points, expressed in degrees and measured from the 1st section base vector towards the 2nd section base vector.
-# \confdef{section_default,logical} is a flag to request default or non-default section definition and yield locus scaling.
If \conffield{section_default} equals
  - `True` <BR> then the yield locus section is defined by \f$\sigma_{11}\f$ (normal stress along RD) along the X-axis, and \f$\sigma_{22}\f$ (normal stress along TD) along the Y-axis. Furthermore, the yield locus will be scaled by the yield stress corresponding to uniaxial tension along the RD.
  - `False` <BR> then the following suboptions are required:
    -# \confdef{normalize_base,logical}. If `True`, then the base tensors specified by the following configuration 	settings 2.-3.-4. are normalized so their norm is equal to 1.0.
	-# \confdef{base_sigma_X,symsrtensor} is the base tensor along the X-axis of the yield locus section, in [Voigt notation](@ref symsrtensor_option).
	-# \confdef{base_sigma_Y,symsrtensor} is the base tensor along the Y-axis of the yield locus section, in [Voigt notation](@ref symsrtensor_option).
	-# \confdef{base_sigma_offset,symsrtensor} is the base tensor that determines the mode of the stress that coincides with the origin of the yield locus section, in [Voigt notation](@ref symsrtensor_option).
	-# \confdef{normalize_stress,logical}
	-# \confdef{use_scaling,logical}. If it equals
	  - `False` <BR> then the suboption
        -# \confdef{scaling_stress_mode,symsrtensor} is the stress mode for which the yield point is used as scaling factor, in [Voigt notation](@ref symsrtensor_option).

\par Example
Please see the configuration file `AKDQf_yld.cfg` from `examples` directory:
\includelineno AKDQf_yld.cfg

\par Example
Please see the configuration file `sid1687f_yld.cfg` from `examples` directory:
\includelineno sid1687f_yld.cfg




\subsection output_yld Module-specific output

The module-specific outputfile `<output_prefix>.xyld` makes column-wise reporting of:
<ol>
	<li> \outdef{theta,yldout_theta} The angle \f$ \theta \f$ corresponding to a yield locus
	point, as requested in \conffield{theta_range}, expressed in degrees.
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{w} The multiplier for the yield locus offset tensor, as requested in
	\conffield{offset_range}
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{sigma} The radial coordinate of the yield locus point
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{sigma_scaled} The radial coordinate of the normalized yield locus point
	</li>

	<li> \outref{S, norm_SonA}
	</li>

	<li> \outref{dotW}
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{sigma_x} The 1st Cartesian coordinate of the normalized yield locus point
	\f$ \sigma_X \f$
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{sigma_y} The 2nd Cartesian coordinate of the normalized yield locus point
	\f$ \sigma_Y \f$
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{dsigma_x} The 1st Cartesian coordinate of the local yield locus normal
	\f$ A_X \f$
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{dsigma_y} The 2nd Cartesian coordinate of the local yield locus normal
	\f$ A_Y \f$
	</li>

	<li> \outdef{beta} The angle \f$ \beta \f$ from X-axis to the projection of tensor
	\f$ \mathbf{A} \f$ into section, expressed in degrees.
	It is given as \f$ \beta= \arctan(\frac{A_Y}{A_X}) \f$.
	</li>

	<li> \outref{residual}
	</li>
</ol>

\section module_adp Arbirtary Deformation Path (keyword: ADP)

The Arbitrary Deformation Path prescribes an arbitrary generic deformation on a virtual
sample. Several subsequent deformation _steps_ may be imposed; each step imposes a certain
(constant) deformation. The module can also be used to simply calculate stress response of
the virtual sample to a superimposed deformation. All inputs and outputs are reported in the
global material frame.

\note This module implements a deformation-driven crystal plasticity model. The only
available option in \ref config_search_procedure is to use the default settings.

\subsection configuration_adp Module-specific configuration

-# \confdef{n_steps,integer,n_steps_ADP} sets the number of subsequent deformation steps,
   each one being specified by later configuration settings and their optional
   subsettings. These subsettings are repeated \conffield{n_steps} times in configuration
   file, in the same order as they are to be applied to the virtual sample:
    -# \confdef{Velocity_gradient,srtensor_voigt} Velocity gradient tensor expressed in [Voigt notation](@ref srtensor_option_voigt)

    -# \confdef{strain_magnitude,real} Magnitude of strain to be imposed.

---

\par Example
Please see the configuration file `sid1687f_adp.cfg` from `examples` directory:
\includelineno sid1687f_adp.cfg

\subsection output_adp Module-specific output

The module-specific outputfile `<output_prefix>.adp` makes column-wise reporting of:
-# \outdef{step,step_ADP} The deformation step sequence number


-# \outdef{increment,increment_ADP} The increment sequence number of the current
   deformation step

-# \outdef{duration,duration_ADP} The duration of the increment.

-# \outdef{S_11,adp_S_11} The 11 component of the deviatoric stress, given in the material
   (123) reference frame. The deviatoric stress \f$ \mathbf{S} \f$ corresponds to the
   imposed \f$ \mathbf{L} \f$.
-# \outdef{S_22,adp_S_22} similar to \ref adp_S_11 "S_11"
-# \outdef{S_33,adp_S_33} similar to \ref adp_S_11 "S_11"
-# \outdef{S_12,adp_S_12} similar to \ref adp_S_11 "S_11"
-# \outdef{S_23,adp_S_23} similar to \ref adp_S_11 "S_11"
-# \outdef{S_13,adp_S_13} similar to \ref adp_S_11 "S_11"

-# \outdef{eps_vM_end} Von Mises strain at the end of the increment

-# \outdef{M} Taylor factor of the last increment in the step.

---

